markdown <<MARKDOWN
<div style="font-size:14pt; line-height:1.4;">

# Promise chain vs. async/await
  
#### You already know how to use a Promise chain with <code style="font-size:12pt;">.then()</code>.

#### In this level, you’ll compare two versions of the *same* logic: one written with a Promise chain and one written with <code style="font-size:12pt;">async</code>/<code style="font-size:12pt;">await</code>.

#### Your job is to explain what looks different and why the behavior stays the same.
 

<div style="background:#f7f8fa; border-radius:10px; padding:14px; margin:12px 0; box-sizing:border-box;">
    <p style="margin:0; font-size:14pt;">
      💡 <strong>Key idea:</strong> <code style="font-size:12pt;">async</code>/<code style="font-size:12pt;">await</code> does not replace Promises — it’s a cleaner way to write
      Promise logic so it reads like step-by-step code.
    </p>
  </div>



<hr style="margin:12px 0;" />

## Let's Compare

<div style="font-size:14pt; line-height:1.4;">

<div style="display:flex; gap:16px; flex-wrap:wrap; justify-content:center; align-items:flex-start; margin-top:10px;">

<!-- LEFT CARD -->
 <div style="background:#ffffff; border:1px solid #ddd; border-radius:12px; padding:14px; width:440px; height:550px; box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.08);">

<div style="font-size:14pt; font-weight:bold; margin-bottom:10px;">
      Version A: Promise Chain (.then())
    </div>

<!-- BLACK CODE BOX -->
<div style="background-color:#000000; border-radius:12px; padding:18px; margin-bottom:14px; overflow-x:auto;">

<pre style="margin:0; background-color:#000000; font-family:Consolas, Monaco, 'Courier New', monospace; font-size:14px; line-height:1.5; color:#f8f8f2;">

<span style="color:#4dabf7;">fetch</span>(<span style="color:#69db7c;">url</span>)
  .<span style="color:#ffd43b;">then</span>((response) => {
    <span style="color:#ff6b6b;">return</span> response.<span style="color:#4dabf7;">json</span>();
  })
  .<span style="color:#ffd43b;">then</span>((data) => {
    <span style="color:#4dabf7;">console</span>.<span style="color:#4dabf7;">log</span>(data);
  });

</pre>
 </div>

<!-- HOW TO READ -->
<div style="font-size:14pt;">
 <strong>How to read it:</strong>
  <ul style="margin-top:8px;">
        <li style="font-size:14pt;"><code style="font-size:12pt;">fetch(url)</code> starts a request and returns a <strong>Promise</strong>.</li>
        <li style="font-size:14pt;">The first <code style="font-size:12pt;">.then()</code> runs after the response is ready and returns <code>response.json()</code>.</li>
        <li style="font-size:14pt;">The second <code style="font-size:12pt;">.then()</code> runs after the JSON is ready and gives you <code style="font-size:12pt;">data</code>.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

  </div>

<!-- RIGHT CARD -->
<div style="background:#ffffff; border:1px solid #ddd; border-radius:12px; padding:14px; width:440px;height:550px;  box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.08);">

<div style="font-size:14pt; font-weight:bold; margin-bottom:10px;">
      Version B: async / await
    </div>

<!-- BLACK CODE BOX -->
<div style="background-color:#000000; border-radius:12px; padding:18px; margin-bottom:14px; overflow-x:auto;">

<pre style="margin:0; background-color:#000000; font-family:Consolas, Monaco, 'Courier New', monospace; font-size:14px; line-height:1.5; color:#f8f8f2;">

<span style="color:#ff6b6b;">async</span> <span style="color:#ff6b6b;">function</span> <span style="color:#4dabf7;">getData</span>() {

  <span style="color:#ff6b6b;">const</span> response =
    <span style="color:#ff6b6b;">await</span> <span style="color:#4dabf7;">fetch</span>(<span style="color:#69db7c;">url</span>);

  <span style="color:#ff6b6b;">const</span> data =
    <span style="color:#ff6b6b;">await</span> response.<span style="color:#4dabf7;">json</span>();

  <span style="color:#4dabf7;">console</span>.<span style="color:#4dabf7;">log</span>(data);

}

</pre>
</div>

<!-- HOW TO READ -->
<div style="font-size:14pt;">
<strong>How to read it:</strong>
<ul style="margin-top:8px;">
        <li style="font-size:14pt;"><code style="font-size:12pt;">await fetch(url)</code> pauses <em>this async function</em> until the Promise resolves.</li>
        <li style="font-size:14pt;"><code style="font-size:12pt;">await response.json()</code> pauses again because JSON parsing also returns a Promise.</li>
        <li style="font-size:14pt;">Then the next line runs, using <code style="font-size:12pt;">data</code> like normal step-by-step code.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

  </div>

</div>

</div>

  </div>

  <hr style="margin:12px 0;" />

## What’s different (and what stays the same)?

<ul style="margin:0 0 12px 20px; padding:0;">
    <li style="margin:0 0 6px 0; font-size:14pt;">
      <strong>Different structure:</strong> The Promise chain uses nested callback functions inside <code style="font-size:12pt;">.then(...)</code>.
      The <code style="font-size:12pt;">async</code>/<code>await</code> version removes most nesting and reads top-to-bottom.
    </li>
    <li style="margin:0 0 6px 0;font-size:14pt;">
      <strong>Same timing:</strong> Both versions still rely on Promises and still run asynchronously. Nothing “became synchronous.”
    </li>
    <li style="margin:0 0 6px 0; font-size:14pt;">
      <strong>Same steps:</strong> (1) request data, (2) wait for the response, (3) convert to JSON, (4) use the data.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <div style="background:#B2dee3; border-radius:10px; padding:14px; margin:12px 0; box-sizing:border-box;">
    <p style="margin:0; font-size:14pt;">
      ⭐ <strong>Teacher Tip:</strong> There are <strong>two</strong> waits in both versions.
      <code style="font-size:12pt;">fetch(url)</code> returns a Promise, and <code style="font-size:12pt;">response.json()</code> also returns a Promise.
      That’s why you see either <strong>two <code style="font-size:12pt;">.then()</code></strong> calls or <strong>two <code style="font-size:12pt;">await</code></strong> lines.
    </p>
  </div>

## Quick Check

<ol style="margin:0 0 0 20px; padding:0;">
<li style="margin:0 0 8px 0; font-size:14pt;">
      What replaced <code style="font-size:12pt;">.then()</code> in Version B?
    </li>
    <li style="margin:0 0 8px 0; font-size:14pt;">
      Why are there still two <code style="font-size:12pt;">await</code> lines?
    </li>
    <li style="margin:0 0 8px 0; font-size:14pt;">
      Does Version B change what the program does, or only how it’s written? Explain.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <details style="background-color:#ececec; padding:10px; margin-top:12px; border:1px solid transparent; border-radius:8px;">
    <summary style="font-size:14pt; font-weight:bold;">💡 Check your thinking (click to reveal)</summary>
<div style="margin-top:12px; margin-left:14px; margin-right:14px; margin-bottom:10px; font-size:14pt; line-height:1.4;">

<ul style="margin:0 0 0 20px; padding:0;">
<li style="margin:0 0 8px 0; font-size:14pt;">
          <strong>What replaced <code style="font-size:12pt;">.then()</code>?</strong><br />
          Each <code style="font-size:12pt;">await</code> is doing the same job as a <code>.then()</code>: “wait for this Promise to finish, then continue.”
        </li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 8px 0; font-size:14pt;">
          <strong>Why two awaits?</strong><br />
          Because there are two Promises: one from <code>fetch(url)</code> (wait for the response) and one from <code style="font-size:12pt;">response.json()</code> (wait for the data).
        </li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 8px 0; font-size:14pt;">
          <strong>Did the behavior change?</strong><br />
          No — both versions do the same steps in the same order and still run asynchronously. The difference is readability.
        </li>
      </ul>
 </div>
  </details>

</div>



---


## Next 

